an</surname> <given-names>AS</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Lillibridge</surname> <given-names>SR</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Ostroff</surname> <given-names>SM</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Hughes</surname> <given-names>JM</given-names></string-name> <article-title>Public health assessment of potential biological terrorism agents.</article-title><source>Emerg Infect Dis</source> <year>2002</year>;<volume>8</volume>:<fpage>225</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>30</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11897082</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R9"><label>9. </label><citation citation-type="journal"><string-name><surname>Lazarus</surname> <given-names>R</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Kleinman</surname> <given-names>K</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Dashevsky</surname> <given-names>I</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Adams</surname> <given-names>C</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>Kludt</surname> <given-names>P</given-names></string-name>, <string-name><surname>DeMaria</surname> <given-names>A</given-names> <suffix>Jr</suffix></string-name>, <etal/><article-title>Use of automated ambulatory-care encounter records for detection of acute illness clusters, including potential bioterrorism events.</article-title><source>Emerg Infect Dis</source> <year>2002</year>;<volume>8</volume>:<fpage>753</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>60</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12141958</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R10"><label>10. </label><citation citation-type="journal"><string-name><surname>Buckeridge</surname> <given-names>DL</given-names></string-name> <article-title>Outbreak detection through automated surveillance: a review of the determinants of detection.</article-title><source>J Biomed Inform</source> <year>2007</year>;<volume>40</volume>:<fpage>370</fpage>&#x02013;<lpage>9</lpage> <pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.jbi.2006.09.003</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid